{
  "image_name": "4b4b2dfcbad61e17dc57bde2cba57724.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how cells in the tumor microenvironment suppress anti-tumor immunity and limit immune-cell access to the tumor. At the center, tumor-associated macrophages (TAMs) receive signals such as M-CSF and adenosine and then release factors including TGFβ, ARG, IDO, and IL-10. These signals inhibit NK cells and cytotoxic T lymphocytes, reducing cytotoxic responses against tumor cells. TAMs also appear to act on blood vessels by lowering diapedesis or extravasation, which would restrict immune cells from crossing the endothelium, and they promote fibroblast-like CAF activity and extracellular matrix buildup, further decreasing infiltration.\n\nThe lower part of the figure shows additional suppressive myeloid circuits. Dendritic cells, influenced by pathways involving WNT, HGF/MET, and LRP, promote regulatory T-cell differentiation and suppress effector T cells through IDO and IL-10. Myeloid-derived suppressor cells (MDSCs) inhibit T cells using ARG, nitric oxide, and reactive oxygen species, and also enhance Treg-mediated suppression through TGFβ, IDO, and TDO2. Overall, the image depicts a coordinated network in which macrophages, dendritic cells, MDSCs, CAFs, and Tregs create a hostile environment for effective immune-cell infiltration and tumor killing.",
